# Example crontab entries for seclog. These are EXAMPLES: nothing installs them for you.
# Install by running "crontab -e" as the user that should run the analysis and pasting the
# lines you want. Adjust the paths to where you cloned the repository.
#
#  minute hour day-of-month month day-of-week  command
#
# The user must be allowed to read the log. On Debian/Ubuntu that means being a member of
# the "adm" group (an administrator adds you; seclog never changes permissions itself).

# 1) Analyse yesterday's authentication log every day at 06:15 and keep one report per day.
15 6 * * *  /opt/seclog/scripts/run_daily.sh --input /var/log/auth.log --output-base "$HOME/seclog-reports" >> "$HOME/seclog-reports/cron.log" 2>&1

# 2) Same, but ignore the internal vulnerability scanner so it does not trigger SL-001.
# 15 6 * * *  /opt/seclog/scripts/run_daily.sh --exclude-ips 10.10.30.60 >> "$HOME/seclog-reports/cron.log" 2>&1

# 3) Exit status 10 (a high severity finding) can feed a notification. Replace "mail" with
#    whatever you use. Note: run_daily.sh does not pass --fail-on-findings; call seclog directly.
# 30 6 * * *  /opt/seclog/bin/seclog analyze -i /var/log/auth.log --since "$(date -d yesterday +\%F)" --until "$(date -d yesterday +\%F)" -o "$HOME/seclog-reports/high-$(date +\%F)" --fail-on-findings -q || mail -s "seclog: high severity findings" you@example.test < /dev/null

# Cron specifics worth knowing:
#  - "%" must be written as "\%" inside a crontab line (see the date commands above).
#  - Cron starts jobs with a minimal PATH, so absolute paths are used.
#  - Output of the job is mailed to the user unless it is redirected, as in example 1.
